AWK/gawk, JQ, Query Tabular, SQLite, SQL-like tools, or wrappers that accept\narbitrary code as the computation payload.\n\n### Local Code Allowlist\n\n**Before upload:** Inspect metadata, headers, dimensions, and archive members\nwhen needed for staging; extract unchanged archive members; perform lossless\nformat conversion only when Galaxy cannot ingest the original format.\n\n**After Galaxy:** From downloaded Galaxy outputs only, select rows or columns,\nsort, apply thresholds to Galaxy-computed values, count rows, calculate simple\nsummaries or ratios from Galaxy-derived values, round, and format the answer.\nLocal extraction may read already-computed scalar values or attributes from a\nGalaxy output, but must not fit, predict, optimize, or perform other scientific\ncomputation.\n\nIf removing all Galaxy steps would still let local code produce the answer,\nredo the computation on Galaxy.\n\n### Route Selection\n\nUse ordinary Galaxy tools or workflows, or a fresh UDT. Choose the shortest\nroute that faithfully implements the method and quantity requested by the\ntask. Do not silently replace a named method, model, estimator, metric, or data\nsource with a different one. A search result is only a compact candidate: when\nit appears relevant, inspect that tool before rejecting the ordinary-tool\nroute. Use a UDT only when no plausible candidate exists or targeted inspection\nshows that the required operation is unavailable.\n\n### Galaxy Access\n\nUse `search_galaxy_tools` with concise tool-family, operation, package, or\ntarget-metric terms for live ordinary-tool discovery; do not recreate tool\nsearch with BioBlend `get_tools()`. Use the available Galaxy MCP execution tools\nfor supported inspection, submission, and blocking execution. In\nparticular, use `run_galaxy_tool_and_wait` for ordinary tools and\n`run_galaxy_udt_and_wait` to create and run a UDT. Do not replace these\nsupported operations with BioBlend tool creation, submission, status queries,\nor polling. Use BioBlend only when no available Galaxy MCP tool covers the\noperation.\n\nFor ordinary tools and UDTs, make one blocking execution call with an appropriate\ntotal timeout, normally 5400 seconds. Validation, submission, and terminal job\nfailures return directly; `new` or `queued` is not a failure. Do not query the\nsame job or submit a duplicate while that call is active. Replace a nonterminal\njob only when a concrete queue, destination, or route problem justifies it, and\ncancel the old job before submitting the replacement.\n\n### Output\n\nWrite `codex_output/answer.txt` only after the requested quantity has been\ncomputed successfully. The file must contain exactly one canonical answer line:\n\n- Numbers: decimal or scientific notation without thousands separators.\n- Percentages: append `%` only when the question requests a percentage.\n- Strings, genes, chromosomes, directions, categories, and choices: use the\n  exact requested label.\n- Do not include prose, Markdown, alternatives, citations, units labels, or\n  caveats.\n\nDo not read files outside the workspace except installed skill instructions\nunder `$CODEX_HOME/skills` and `GALAXY_API_KEY_FILE`; load the key directly and\nnever print it.\n---\n{\n  \"tag\": \"bixbench\",\n  \"version\": \"1.5\",\n  \"question\": \"What is the mean of per-chromosome densities (CpGs per base pair) for filtered (>90% or <10% methylation) unique age-related CpGs across all chromosomes (only including those with at least one filtered CpG) in the Jackdaw genome?\",\n  \"categories\": \"Epigenomics\",\n  \"source\": \"BixBench-Verified-50\",\n  \"ground_truth_visible\": false\n}\n---\n{\n  \"seed_history_id\": \"bbd44e69cb8906b59a30f648c46d2fac\",\n  \"seed_history_name\": \"Seed: What is the mean of per-chromosome densities (CpGs per base pair) for filtered (>90% or <10% methylation) unique age-related CpGs across all chromosomes (only including\",\n  \"input_count\": 4,\n  \"history_url\": \"https://usegalaxy.or